Converting non-bonded parameters from LAMMPS to AMBER format requires careful attention because the two engines use different functional forms, units, and formatting styles for Lennard-Jones (LJ) interactions.Here is a guide on how to convert these parameters and map them correctly.1. Understand the Functional Forms & UnitsThe biggest hurdle in this conversion is that LAMMPS typically uses the A/B coefficient form or standard distance/energy variables, while AMBER relies on $\sigma$ (or $R_{min}/2$) and $\epsilon$.LAMMPS (pair_style lj/cut)LAMMPS usually defines the LJ potential using $\epsilon$ (energy well depth) and $\sigma$ (collision diameter):$$V(r) = 4\epsilon \left[ \left(\frac{\sigma}{r}\right)^{12} - \left(\frac{\sigma}{r}\right)^6 \right]$$Units (real): $\epsilon$ is in kcal/mol, $\sigma$ is in Angstroms.LAMMPS Alternative (pair_style lj/class2): Uses the 9-6 potential instead of 12-6. Note: AMBER natively uses 12-6, so 9-6 parameters cannot be directly converted without refitting.AMBER (frcmod or prmtop)AMBER expresses the 12-6 potential using $A$ and $B$ coefficients internally, but in the parameter files (frcmod), it uses $R_{min}/2$ (the radius at the potential minimum) and $\epsilon$:$$V(r) = \epsilon \left[ \left(\frac{R_{min}}{r}\right)^{12} - 2\left(\frac{R_{min}}{r}\right)^6 \right]$$Units: $\epsilon$ is in kcal/mol, $R_{min}/2$ is in Angstroms.2. The Conversion EquationsSince $\epsilon$ is identical in both formats (assuming LAMMPS real units), you only need to convert LAMMPS $\sigma$ to AMBER $R_{min}/2$.The relationship between the distance at the zero-energy point ($\sigma$) and the distance at the minimum-energy point ($R_{min}$) is:$$R_{min} = \sigma \cdot 2^{1/6} \approx \sigma \cdot 1.12246$$Because AMBER expects the half-distance ($R_{min}/2$) for individual atom types, the final conversion formula is:$$\frac{R_{min}}{2} = \frac{\sigma \cdot 2^{1/6}}{2} = \sigma \cdot 2^{-5/6} \approx \sigma \cdot 0.56123$$⚠️ Crucial Warning on Combining RulesAMBER strictly uses Lorentz-Berthelot combining rules:$$\sigma_{ij} = \frac{\sigma_i + \sigma_j}{2} \quad \text{and} \quad \epsilon_{ij} = \sqrt{\epsilon_i \cdot \epsilon_j}$$LAMMPS can use geometric, arithmetic, or explicitly defined pair coefficients (pair_coeff).If your LAMMPS simulation used pair_modify mix geometric, a direct conversion to AMBER will introduce errors because AMBER cannot natively mix Lennard-Jones parameters geometrically without manual off-diagonal overrides (e.g., using NONB modifications in modern AMBER).3. Formatting for AMBER (frcmod)To introduce these converted parameters into AMBER, you will typically write an frcmod (force field modification) file.The non-bonded section in an frcmod file comes under the NONBON header. Here is the exact format required:PlaintextRemark line: put whatever you want here
